<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kulottunga I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1070–<a href="/years/1122">1122</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Kulothunga Chola I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kulottunga I was emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> from 1070 to 1122. Born Rajendra, son of the Eastern Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-narendra">Rajaraja Narendra</a> and a Chola princess, he united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> kingdom with the Chola realm on taking the Chola throne. His long reign was a time of stability, and he is remembered for abolishing tolls and for diplomatic relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i.jpg" alt="Kulottunga I" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthew T Rader · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sculpture_in_a_wall_at_Nataraja_Temple_in_Chidambaram,_Tamil_Nadu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kulottunga, whose name means the Exalter of His Clan, was born around 1025 under the star of Pusya. His mother Ammangadevi was a daughter of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a>, and his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-narendra">Rajaraja Narendra</a> was himself the son of Kundavai, a daughter of Rajaraja Chola I. The poem Kalingattuparani describes his birth as an incarnation of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and says he was brought up at Rajendra's court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangaikondacholapuram">Gangaikondacholapuram</a>.</p><p>As a prince he fought in the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a> and his successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajadhiraja-chola">Rajadhiraja I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-ii">Rajendra Chola II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virarajendra-chola">Virarajendra Chola</a>. In his inscriptions he claims that, while still heir-apparent, he captured herds of elephants at Vayiragaram, conquered Sakkarakottam, the Chakrakota region around modern Bastar, and took tribute from its Nagavanshi ruler Dharavarsha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Kulottunga succeeded his cousin Athirajendra Chola in 1070. That year Vijayabahu of Lanka attacked the Chola forces on the island from Rohana and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a>; after further indecisive fighting the Cholas withdrew. On the mainland Kulottunga fought the Western Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> and then campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, who had used the confusion of his accession to reassert their independence.</p><p>His reign saw wide overseas contacts. An embassy of 1077 to the Chinese court, which the historian Nilakanta Sastri attributes to him, returned with more than 81,000 strings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> cash, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a> presented him with a precious stone in 1114. An inscription of 1079 in a Taoist temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> calls him supreme chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>. At home he carried out a land survey that became the basis of taxation, replaced the Chola-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> viceroys in the south with military cantonments, and with his son enlarged the Nataraja Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chidambaram">Chidambaram</a> sixfold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kulottunga died around 1122, at about 97, though this is disputed, and was succeeded by his son Vikrama Chola. Late in his reign the northern half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> seems to have passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a>, and some historians hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangavadi">Gangavadi</a> was lost to the Hoysala Vishnuvardhana. The poet Jayamkondar composed the Kalingattuparani at his court. Historians dispute whether he was Krimikanta Chola, the persecutor of the Vaishnavite acharya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>, and his records also show gifts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> shrines. Nilakanta Sastri judged that he avoided unnecessary wars and that his reign laid the foundation for the empire's well-being over the next 150 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kulottunga I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was ruled by a hereditary dynasty whose line changed in 1070, when Athirajendra Chola was assassinated in a civil disturbance and <b>Kulothunga Chola I</b>, son of the Eastern Chalukya prince Rajaraja Narendra and the Chola princess Ammanga Devi, took the throne, beginning the Later Chola dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Vikramaditya VI was more successful: he exploited the troubles of the Chola king <b>Kulottunga I</b> to occupy Vengi c. 1118, and his generals contained the rising power of the Hoysalas under Vishnuvardhana.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1070 the Eastern Chalukya prince Rajendra became Chola emperor as <b>Kulottunga I</b>, and Vengi was merged into the Chola realm, the last viceroys ruling until about 1075.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 11th century they rose as feudatories of the Chola emperor <b>Kulottunga I</b>, who had absorbed the lands of the Eastern Chalukyas, and governed the Vengi region on his behalf from Chandavolu, near Tenali.</blockquote><a href="/state/velanati-chodas">Velanati Chodas</a></li></ul></section>
